# homma

homma orchestrates a multi-repository workspace: it knows which repositories the
workspace contains, who works in it, where each participant's work happens, and
how they correspond.

It is deliberately small. Designing and building inside a repository belongs to
that repository's own tooling; homma's job is what spans repositories and what is
true of the workspace rather than of any one member.

## What it must be

**General.** homma runs against a workspace it has never seen. **No key is
required**: the content repository defaults to the configuration's own directory,
and every path has a default homma will create plus an override for a workspace
whose convention differs. So a fresh directory with a registry in it is already a
workspace. Nothing about the workspace homma was born in is compiled in, which is
why layout, roles and record kinds are data rather than code.

Membership follows from that. A directory under the workspace root that is its
own repository is a member, and nothing lists it. Which forge it lives on and
who owns it come from its `origin` remote, which is the thing that decides
where a push lands and is therefore the only honest source for either. A
workspace that renames a repository, clones a new one, or drops one is correct
the moment the tree is, with nothing left saying otherwise.

**Minimal, and delegating.** Where a member repository has its own tooling, homma
drives it rather than reimplementing it. Releasing is the case where the tooling
is the workspace's own: a release is a merge of `dev` onto `main` tagged,
published and announced as one act, gated by a run on the developer's machine
that the forge is told about, and homma drives that because it is the one thing
every workspace has at hand.

## The four primitives

Everything else is a view over these, a kind declared in configuration, or prose
in a generated file.

**The record store.** One engine for everything homma persists. Kinds declare
what attributes a record has and whether it may be edited, so correspondence and
structural work share a write path and differ by a flag.

**The daemon.** Sole writer of the store, sole waker of participants, sole arbiter
of who may act on whose behalf. Three verbs: append a record, deliver what is
waiting, grant a dispatch.

**The registry.** Identity as configuration rather than as a process, so a
participant outlives whatever was running it.

**The generation pass.** Configuration and templates into definitions, settings,
hooks and links; the sole writer of the agent configuration in every workspace it
manages, **the workspace's own rules, skills and agents included**.

## The layers

```
L0 contracts     types and traits, no I/O
L1 mechanism     git, forge, filesystem. things with side effects
L2 store         records and their persistence
L3 domain        registry, correspondence, work
L4 service       the daemon and its surfaces
L5 surface       what a human or an agent drives
```

Crates arrive when something needs them. An empty crate holding a name is a
directory pretending to be a decision.

## What exists

`homma-api` carries the vocabulary. `homma-store` persists records.
`homma-org` holds the registry, the workspace lifecycle and generation.
Its registry is `OrgRegistry`, a remote it has parsed is `ParsedRemote` and what a config check found
is `ConfigFinding`, because the core and the api hold a `Registry`, a `Remote` and a `Finding` of their
own and one name has one definition.
`homma-engine` is the command surface, and `homma` is the launcher that finds
the workspace, reads the version of the engine it pins, and runs that one.
`homma-core` still holds forge clients and git operations in a pre-adoption
split that a later round dissolves, and it is where the release lives: the gate
that measures a commit, the run record and commit status it leaves, and the
merge, tag, changelog, GitHub release and registry publish that carry `dev`
onto `main`. `homma release` in `homma-engine` is its surface, and
`CORE_RELEASE.md` is the whole of it. `homma milestone` is the sibling that tells where
the current milestone began and reports on the range since, and `CORE_MILESTONE.md` is the
whole of that.

## The rule corpus

A workspace's rules are injected into every session it runs, sub-agents
included, so their size is paid before any work starts. They are authored as
templates and generated, like everything else the generation pass owns.

**One file per rule, and the card is its opening.** The meta as frontmatter, then
the card, then a marker, then the elaboration; the always-loaded card is that
prefix, written by the pass and never by hand. **The card is a prefix of the full
rule rather than a second document**, so nothing is written twice and the two
cannot drift.

Two files were tried first. Frontmatter on one of a pair governs the other
silently, so opening the card template showed none of the meta shaping it, and an
elaboration could lose the card beside it and be authored, findable and absent
from every session at once. **That error class is gone rather than guarded.**

**The meta is what makes a rule findable without its name.** Topics stated in the
words a reader arrives with, and the moment the rule fires. A session about to
write a README asks the subject and gets the governing set; asking by filename
only works for somebody who already knows the answer.

A card whose rule is gone is reported and not deleted. The destination is the
always-loaded set, so such a card is not a stale file sitting in a directory,
it is a document injected into every session for as long as it is there, and
saying nothing about it is the one thing the pass may not do. Deleting it is
the other thing: on disk a card the pass wrote last time and a document
somebody put there on purpose are the same file, and this is the case the skill
corpus below already decides for a directory nothing claims.

**A card is for a session, and not for every session.** Two more optional keys
state whom a rule binds. `roles` is a list of `coordinator` and `helper`, and
absent means every role, and so does an empty list; a role named twice is read
as listed. `where` is `cloud` or `local`, and absent means both.
A role here is the session's own, which is not the standing a person has in the
organisation. The session is the role found in `~/.config/clause-dev/role`, the
file the setup script and the bootstrap write, and the place, which is cloud
when `CLAUSE_CLOUD` is `1` and local otherwise; an explicit option on the
command overrides each. A session with no role file takes every role, so
nothing is withheld from it for want of a role, and its place is still read from
the cloud variable. A home directory that is not set means there is no role file.
A role file
holding any other word is refused by name, since taking every role would hide a
misspelt file behind a full load.

A card the session excludes is not written. One that an earlier pass wrote for
an authored rule and that this session excludes is removed, because the rule
claims it and the pass owns it, which is not the case of a card nothing claims.
Discovery is not shaped by the session: `rules about` and `rules show` answer
for every rule, so a card left out is one command away.

An element of an inline list may carry a comma inside quotes, and the split
respects them. A brace group is the ordinary way to write a path pattern, so a
list that split on every comma could not express one at all.

## The skill corpus

The same split, one tier over. A skill is authored as a directory rather than a
file, and what it holds beside its manifest is reference prose, scripts and
sometimes a command-line tool of its own.

**A template is rendered and everything else is copied with its mode.** A shell
script is not prose, and templating one would mean it could not carry a brace
without escaping it.

**The bound is on the description, not on the body.** A body is fetched when a
session reaches for the skill and costs nothing until then; every description is
carried on every session, because the listing is how a session tells which skill
to reach for. An over-budget description is dropped rather than refused, which
leaves the skill named and silent about when it applies.

**Which skills bear on a rule is derived rather than declared**, from citations
in both directions, because a hand-kept list goes wrong the first time a skill is
renamed and nothing reports it.

## The agent corpus

The same split for the personas a session can dispatch. One persona is one
file, authored as `<name>.md.tmpl` and generated into the directory the host
reads its agents from, where it is never edited by hand. **The name it declares
is its filename**, and one that differs is refused, since the host dispatches by
the declared name and a reader finds the file by the other.

A persona is prose with frontmatter, so the whole file is a template, and one
that does not render is refused with its path. The frontmatter is the host's,
may nest, and is written out as it was; of it only the top-level `name` and
`description` are read, each as a value on its own line, and a folded or
literal value or a trailing comment on either is refused rather than read as
something the author did not write, since reading those properly is a YAML
parser the rest of the corpus declines to carry. A generated persona whose source
is gone is reported and not deleted, for the reason the rule corpus gives: on
disk it is the same as a file somebody put there, and a persona left in the
listing is offered to every session until somebody says so.

## The command surface

```
homma org        who exists
homma work       workspaces and what is in them
homma rules      the corpus: what governs a subject, generating the cards of a session, and what each kind of session loads
homma skills     what is authored, and generating the tree a session finds
homma agents     the personas: what is authored, and generating the ones a session dispatches
homma identity   the identities: generating a character's agent definition, and starting a session as it
homma sauna      correspondence and work
homma serve      the daemon and its surfaces
homma release    the gate, the run record, and dev onto main as a release
homma milestone  where the current milestone began, and the reports over the range since
homma capture    what the person said to a session, copied into the capture store
homma answers    what was left on a page or a channel, into the capture store and onto the rows it answers
homma tools      the content repository's tools, and the promoted ones as commands
homma what       which tool, script, verb, skill or rule does a thing, found by words
```

`homma capture --title <title>` reads an agent session's transcript and writes
what the person typed into it, and every question put to them with the options
and the answer, as one capture file in the workspace's capture store. The store
is `captures` under `[paths]` in the manifest, `.data/op-responses/` in the
content repository when the key is absent, and `--into` names another directory
for one run. The transcript is the one `--session` names, by id or by path; else
the session the command runs inside, which the harness names to every shell it
starts as `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`; else the newest session the harness keeps for
this workspace. The harness keeps them under `~/.claude/projects/`, in a
directory named for the real path the session started in, symlinks resolved,
with every character outside `[A-Za-z0-9]` turned into `-`, counted as the
harness counts them, in UTF-16 units, so a character outside the basic plane
is two. A name that comes out longer than two hundred units is cut at two
hundred and gets `-` and a hash of the whole path after it: each UTF-16 unit
added to thirty-one times the running value, wrapped to 32 bits, its absolute
value written in base 36. The spelling is the harness's own and was read out of
it, so it is checked against that when the harness changes. A session id is
looked for in the workspace's directory first and then in every other one there,
since a session started inside a member repository keeps its transcript under
that repository's name; an id found nowhere is refused rather than replaced by
another session. A last line with no newline is still being written and is not
read.

A write to the harness's file is sometimes left cut off inside a string, and the
next line is written straight after it before any newline: one line of the file
then holds the start of an object and a complete object after it. The file does
not say what made the write stop, and nothing else in it holds the rest. Such a
line is read as the complete object that ends it, taken from the first `{` on
the line from which the rest of it is one object, and the bytes before that are
dropped. What the cut write held is lost with them, and the run says so on
stderr, once for each line it
recovered, naming the line by its number and the bytes dropped, so the loss is
in the output of every run that meets the line while the report on stdout stays
what something parses. The recovered object is an ordinary line from then on,
with its own `uuid` for the watermark and its own place in the order. A line is
read this way only when the bytes before the object begin with `{` and the
parser reaches the end of them with the object it began still open, which is
what a cut write is. Any other line that does not parse is refused by its
number, as any line of a read shape with a field missing is: one with no object
running to its end, one with text before the object that is not the start of
one, and complete lines run together, whether two, or three, or one beside a
cut write, since reading only the last would drop a complete record without a
word.

The words are copied out of the transcript's structured fields and never
rewritten, so every quoted span equals a string the transcript holds. A line is
the person's when the harness marks its origin as human, since hook output,
compaction summaries, task notifications and prompts the harness sends itself
are all typed lines too and none carries it, unless a tool typed it. A tool that
types into the session's terminal is marked human as well, so such a tool writes
down what it typed beside the transcript, as `<session>.typed-by-tools.ndjson`,
one JSON object per line typed with `at`, the instant before it typed, and
`text`, exactly what it typed. A human line whose words are a recorded `text`,
bare or inside one `<pasted_content id="…">` wrapper the harness puts round a
paste, with nothing outside the wrapper but the line breaks the harness adds
there, and which is stamped at or after that record's `at` and no more than
sixty seconds after it, is the tool's; each record takes at most one line, the
first that fits, so the person typing the same words later is still the person.
A tool writes its record before typing and nothing confirms the typing landed,
so the bound is what keeps a failed paste's record from taking the person's own
words later; a tool's line landing past it is quoted as the person's, which is
visible in the file. A line of the record that does not parse is refused by its
number and nothing on it is recovered, since a record skipped would leave a
tool's line quoted as the person's, and the tool appends one short object a
call. A
message typed while a turn runs is read where it landed: as a typed line once the
turn ended, or as the queued-command attachment the running turn took it in. The
harness can write one message more than once, as an attachment and a typed line
or as two attachments, and ties the copies by `source_uuid`: every human line
whose `uuid` or `source_uuid` another has named is the same message, read where
the first of them was written. Words are a string or a list of content blocks,
the second when an image was pasted beside them, and blocks are read as their
text blocks in order joined by a blank line; an image alone is nothing said. An
answered question is read whole off its tool result, which carries the
questions, their options, the answers and the notes. A tool result is an ask
round when the `tool_use_id` on its content block names a call the agent made to
`AskUserQuestion`, found on an earlier assistant line with that id and that
`name`; a result naming the call of any other tool is not read, whatever keys it
has. A result naming a call that no earlier line holds, which is what a cut
write leaves of the line that carried the question, is read as a round all the
same when its own `toolUseResult` has a round's shape, the `questions` and the
`answers` the harness writes into it, and the run says so on stderr naming the
line, since an answer is the person's words and losing the line that asked must
not lose them. One that has `questions` or `answers` and cannot be read as a
round is not read and is not a refusal, and the run says on stderr which line
holds it and that what it says is not in the capture, so the loss is in the
output. One with neither is nobody's words and passes without a word. A round in
which no question has an answer or a note, the harness's own timeout among them,
said nothing and is not read either.

Only the person's words are blockquotes in the file. The voice tool reads every
blockquote in a capture as one of their remarks, so the agent's text around them,
the question it asked and the turn before an unprompted message, goes in fenced
blocks. That text is copied as it stands, which is longer than a summary and
exact. The agent's fields that sit on one line of the file, the title,
a question's header and an option's label, are written with every line break in
them spelled `\n` and every carriage return `\r`, so none of them can open a
line at the margin.

A capture is not a record homma keeps for itself. It is a file written for
another reader, the voice tool, in that reader's format, which makes it prose in
a generated file rather than something the record store holds; the watermark
below is read back out of those files, so it exists once. Its front matter
carries `tags: [chat]`, the store's mark for words said in a session.

A capture names the session it came from and the `uuid` of the last complete
line the run read in its front matter, as `source` and `through`, and a run takes
only the lines after the latest `through` the store records for it. Latest means
latest in the transcript, and the position in the file is the order everything
follows, since the harness stamps a queued message with the time it was queued
and writes it after lines stamped later. So a second run with nothing new writes
nothing and says so, and there is no state file beside the store to go stale. A
`through` the transcript does not hold is refused rather than guessed around,
except where the transcript is cut: a resumed session in a fresh container gets
its transcript from its last compaction on, so the file opens on the harness's
compaction boundary, and that boundary's `logicalParentUuid` names the last line
before the compaction, which the file lacks. Only that is read as the file being
cut: every boundary the harness writes has no `parentUuid`, so the absence of one
says nothing. A `through` a cut file lacks names a line written before it, and
that capture moves no watermark, since the words it took are not in the file to
be written twice. One exception is known: a message the harness wrote twice, as
an attachment and a typed line or as two attachments, with the first copy before
the cut, reaches the file as the second copy only, and that is taken, since
dropping it would lose the person's words and no line of the file says it was
taken. The run says on stderr how many captures it passed over, beside the lines
it recovered and the results it found no question for, and that a message
written twice across the cut may have been taken again. A
session no capture has come from yet has no watermark, and `--since` takes an
RFC 3339 instant as a floor for that first run, keeping only events stamped after
it; given with a watermark it applies as well, which loses the queued messages
stamped before lines written ahead of them, so it is for the first run only.
With neither the run takes the whole session. On a question that takes one answer,
an answer equal to a label is that choice and anything else is what the person
typed, whole. On one that takes several, an answer is read as the chosen labels
first, joined by `, ` with a label that holds `, ` in double quotes, the longest
label tried first, and whatever follows them is what the person typed. The labels
are the agent's and are named in plain text; the harness's `(notes only)` is
dropped; the typed part and the notes are the person's and are quoted. The file
is created new and never replaces one. One run is one capture, in the order things
happened, and its `kind` says whether it holds unprompted words, answered
questions, or both. The title is given rather than made up, because it decides
whether the voice tool reads the capture at all; `bears_on` is what `--bears-on`
names plus every path the words mention that exists under the workspace root,
and a `--bears-on` holding a line break is refused, since it would write a key of
its own into the front matter; `when` is the first event's local time, and the
file is named for it and the title, lowercased, with `ä` and `å` folded to `a`
and `ö` to `o` and anything else outside `[a-z0-9]` a dash. The store, whether
configured or named by `--into`, is checked against the places homma never
writes, the harness's `~/.claude`, other participants' workspaces and whatever
`deny` names, before anything is written, and that list needs the home directory,
so a run with no `HOME` is refused there even for a session named by path. The
command writes the file and stops. Committing it is the caller's, who is on a
branch in the content repository already.

`homma answers take` is the same kind of command for words left on a page rather
than typed into a session. Its source is `--dir`, a directory holding `answers/`
and `notes/` of JSON documents, or `--channel`, which runs the workspace's `msg`
tool for the messages of kind `answer` and `note` from `--from` (`op` by default)
and takes each body that is a JSON document. A document is checked before
anything is derived from it, because whoever can open the page wrote it: a
timestamp in `at`, an identifier of plain characters in `id`, `key` and `where`,
a `kind` of plain characters (`registry` is the one the engine reads: a bare id on it names the `question` row of that id), text in `asks`, `choice` and `text`, a list
of text in `options`, and no control character in a choice or an option. One
that fails is named on stderr and skipped. A document's store key is
`answers/<id>@<at>` or, for a note, `notes/<digits of at>@<at>`, and one whose key
a capture in the store already lists under `store:` is not taken again, so a run
with nothing new writes nothing. What is new is one capture, `kind` being
`answers`, `notes` or `mixed`, with the questions, every option offered and the
choice in plain words, and what was written as a blockquote, each copied as the
page holds it. Where the words were left is said in the capture and not assumed:
the `tags` and the line under the title read the dashboard page for `--dir`, the
channel for `--channel` and the `registry-append` for the delegate, and the title
names who spoke, `op` for a directory, `--from` for a channel and `someone` for
the delegate. The name is the minute of the latest `at` and the title as a slug,
and a name already taken gets a short hash of the keys. Each answer
whose `key` names a row, `{"ticket": "<id>"}` or a bare id of a `question`, is
attached to it: `cargo mock registry-append <namespace>::<id> answers "<at>:
<capture path>"` with `--local`, in the repository the document's `where` names
when that is a clone in the workspace and in the work's own repository when it
is not. A row that is not there is said on stderr and left, the capture holding
the words. Success prints one line, `took 3 answers 1 note`, and a run with
nothing new prints nothing; `--output json` gives the paths. Committing and
landing is the caller's, as for `homma capture`.

`homma answers delegate <namespace>::<id> <field> <value>` is the program a
workspace's `.mockspace/hooks/registry-append` runs, which mockspace consults
before it writes a row. It takes the step whole only for the field `answers` and
a value of the form `<at>: <words>`: it writes the words as a capture, as an
answer document with no question would be, and attaches `<at>: <capture path>`
to the row with `--local`. The id it gives the document carries a short hash of
the words, so two appends at the same `<at>` with different words are two
documents, and the same words twice are one. A row that cannot be written is a
failure and not a success: the delegate prints why on stderr and exits 1, which
mockspace reports with what it printed, and the capture it wrote is taken away,
so that the same append made again once the row exists is taken afresh and not
dropped as already recorded. For
anything else it exits 3, which leaves the write
to mockspace, so a repository in a workspace with these tools behaves as one
without them for every other field. `homma answers hook` places that file in the
workspace root, naming the engine that wrote it, and `--remove` takes it away;
neither prints anything. The file starts with a test that the engine it names is
still an executable and exits 3 when it is not, so a workspace whose engine was
replaced or removed writes its rows through mockspace as before and not by a hook
that fails every one.

Two commands are the launcher's rather than the engine's, because each runs
where there may be no engine to run. `homma config`, described with the settings
below, is answered by renki for any tool that declares settings, since the
engine is the thing being configured and may not build. `homma workspace` reports the workspace the cwd is
in, or makes one where it is not: bare, inside a workspace, it prints the root
and every repository in it with its branch, whether the tree is dirty and how
many commits sit on no remote; bare, outside one, it clones the content
repository into the cwd, which has to be empty; `spawn <slug>` does the same
under the person's workspaces directory, with the repositories named beside
it; `reap` removes one, refusing while anything in it is dirty or unpushed,
and refusing outright a target that is not a workspace, one the settings
deny, one the cwd is inside, or one holding a worktree, since a seat is
somebody's; `list` names what is there. It is the session's throwaway clone and knows
nothing about identities. The org design's workspace lifecycle, which stands
a participant's workspace up from a registry entry inside a root, is a
different thing and stays in the engine.

What that command needs to know is the person's, not the workspace's, so it
is the first thing homma reads out of a user settings file. The launcher's
descriptor declares four keys through renki's configuration surface, all user
scope: `disallowed_roots`, the directories a workspace may never be made in,
defaulting to the home directory itself with a `/*` suffix denying a whole
subtree; `workspaces_root`, where `spawn <slug>` puts one; `spawn.content_repo`,
the git url a fresh workspace is a clone of, empty by default and refusing to
spawn until set, since a default of the author's own repository is the
author's arrangement compiled in; and `spawn.repos`, what every fresh
workspace clones beside it. `homma config` reads and writes the file, and the
manifest's `deny` list is a different question, where homma may write inside
a workspace rather than where one may be made.

A call with a terminal attached should print for a human, and a call without one
should print its result and nothing else, because something is parsing it. That
split is decided and **not yet built**: nothing in the tree senses a terminal, and
`homma org` prints the same way either way.

Two commands read and write the shared tool configs described in the org
design. `homma repo config check` compares a repository against them and reports;
it exits non-zero when something is missing that the repository is meant to have.
`homma repo config init` places what is missing. They are two commands rather
than one because the check runs on a path that must not write, and placing a
config is a deliberate act.

Which non-zero it is carries the rest of the answer, and the split belongs to
the binary rather than to these two commands. Zero is a command that ran with
nothing to say, `1` is one that ran and reported, and `2` is one that could not
run at all, which is what any error reaching the entry point returns. A caller
has no other way to ask, and the commonest thing behind a `2` here is the
workspace manifest failing to parse, which is nobody's repository's fault.

That path is the gate. A workspace-level hook already stands in front of
`git commit` and `git push` from inside any member repository, works out which
repository the call came from, and refuses when that repository's mockspace
bootstrap is incomplete. It gains the config check, and refuses the same way,
naming the command that fixes it where there is one. Where the check could not
run it says that instead, and names the workspace manifest, because sending
somebody to a command that fails the same way is worse than saying nothing.

What it means by incomplete is a repository that has adopted mockspace, which
is whether it carries a `mockspace.toml`, showing some of mockspace's surfaces
and not others. A repository without the toml has not adopted it, whatever
else it shows, and this check leaves it alone, since the command it would name
has nothing to run there; the shared config check below still applies to it.
Of one that has, none of the surfaces is a repository that never finished
opting in and all of them is one with nothing to say. The mock directory it
looks for is the one the toml declares. Whether the toml is there, which
directory it declares, whether that exists and whether it carries the launcher
pin are asked of `homma agent status`, whose probe reads the toml as a toml, so
the gate holds no reader of its own for a file that two readers would parse
differently. The surfaces it counts are that directory, the launcher pin and the
hooks path; the `cargo mock` alias is not one, since the launcher answers that
command. A missing pin is answered by adding the pin, because `homma agent
regen` runs `cargo mock`, which refuses without one. `core.hooksPath` being set was
one of those surfaces on the reading that mockspace is what sets it, and that
reading is no longer safe: git-shook points the same setting at a dispatcher
directory of its own, in repositories that may never have heard of mockspace,
and the gate then reads one surface of three and refuses every commit there
with an instruction that cannot help. What the gate is asking is whether
mockspace's hooks fire, so a path that is git-shook's answers no and counts as
no, and a path that is anything else counts as before.

The per-repository half is one entrypoint per git event in the repository's
own hooks directory, written by homma and reading its entries off `[hooks]` in
the workspace manifest and the overlay files it lists in `layers`, the release
deep dive has the shape. Where the repository's hooks path is git-shook's,
homma registers through git-shook instead, with a manifest it generates from
every layer into the git directory, so a hook declared only in
`homma.local.toml` is installed and runs. mockspace owns
`core.hooksPath` in every repository it manages, except one where git-shook
already holds it, and homma never contends for it: mockspace's gate runs the
repository's own hook after its own checks, so git's own directory is the one
place both reach, and homma writes there and nowhere else, unless git-shook
holds the key. There each registers an entry of its own, mockspace's
`00-mockspace` and homma's `homma`, and git-shook runs an event's entries in
name order up to the first refusal, so the gate refuses before homma's entry
has run, and a hook git-shook kept as `00-existing` runs ahead of both. Each
registers with `install --yes --only` its own name, so running either is
consent for its own manifest and for no other. The workspace hook stays one file in one place beside that,
which matters because the per-repository wiring is exactly the thing that can
be quietly absent.

An open question for the lead designer: the gate and the status check different
sets of surfaces. The gate counts the mock directory, the launcher pin and the
hooks path; the status asks an adopted repository for the mock directory,
`.claude/`, `.github/instructions/` and the hooks. A repository with a toml and
nothing else is partial in the status and allowed by the gate. Neither set is
declared the right one here, and the two are not unified until he says which.
Three more belong with it. The launcher locates its config by scanning the root
and every immediate subdirectory and treats two as an error, where homma looks
at the root and `mock/` and lets the root win. The status tells a repository
that has the toml and no pin to run `homma agent regen`, which runs `cargo mock`,
which refuses without a pin. A toml that does not parse is counted by the status
as adopting, with the default mock directory and no pin, so the gate refuses it
for a missing pin even where a pin line is there.

The gate asks homma rather than deciding for itself, so the rule that says what
a repository owes exists once. It refuses when it cannot ask at all, for the
same reason it refuses when it cannot read its input: a check that could not run
must never be indistinguishable from one that ran and found nothing.

## The workspace's tools

The content repository carries tools of its own under `tools/<name>/`, each
described by a `tool.toml` in the format renki defines: a `name` that is the
directory's own, a one line `summary`, `tags`, a `backend`, a `source`, a
`promote` flag and one `[[tool.commands]]` table for each verb, with a `summary`
and a `run` that is a path inside the tool and never a shell string. homma is
the host that descriptor is written for, and it is renki's host: a descriptor is
read and checked by `renki::extension`, a tool is found by its `locate` and a
command is built by its `command`, so homma holds no second reading of the
format. What renki refuses, homma refuses for the same reason: a `rev` that is
not a full object name, a url git would read as a flag, a `run` that leaves the
tool, and a command whose file resolves outside the tool through a symlink. It
reads `tools/*/tool.toml` under the workspace root, which is where the manifest
sits, and nothing else is a tool. Two checks are homma's own, because renki does
not make them: the descriptor's name is its directory's, and a verb is named
once.

renki builds on unix only, so the tools are a part of homma that exists on unix
only. Off unix the engine builds without them, and every command that would
reach a tool, `homma tools`, `homma tools check`, `homma tools run`, a built-in
group's word handed on and `homma <tool>`, is refused saying that renki, which
the tools run on, is unix only. No feature turns them off on a platform that can
carry them. On 23 October 2026 op waived the feature half of the per-target and
per-feature gate for these tools, choosing that over a default-off `tools` feature
that workspace builds would turn on.

`homma tools` lists every tool it finds, one line each: the name, the summary,
the verbs, and whether the tool is promoted. A descriptor that cannot be read is
listed by its directory's name with the reason, where a reader sees it, and
`homma tools check` exits non-zero while any is. Listing reads descriptors and
fetches nothing. Running a tool is what finds it, and every backend renki ships
is run: a `local` tool is run from where its `source` names, a path relative to
the workspace root, and a `git` tool is fetched by renki's git backend into the
shared cache once for each revision and run from there. A backend renki has no
row for is refused, and the refusal names the ones it has.

A promoted tool is a subcommand: `homma msg kaski board` runs the tool `msg`.
The first word after the tool's name is a verb when the descriptor names a
command that way, and that command gets the rest. When it does not, and the
descriptor names a command with the tool's own name, that command is the tool's
default and gets everything after the tool's name, which is how a tool whose
first argument is not a verb works. A word that is neither is refused with the
tool's verbs listed. The command is run from the workspace root and replaces
homma, so its input, its output and its exit status are the tool's own, and it
is told where the workspace is in `HOMMA_WORKSPACE` and where the tool's own
files are in `HOMMA_TOOL_ROOT`. A file the command names that is not an
executable file is refused saying which.

`homma tools run <name> <verb> ...` does the same for a tool that is not
promoted, and for one whose name a built-in command already holds.

A built-in command wins over a tool of the same name, except that a built-in
group hands the verbs it does not know to the tool of its name. A group is every
built-in command that takes verbs, `org`, `repo`, `forge`, `agent`, `docs`,
`local`, `rules`, `skills`, `agents`, `release`, `milestone`, `hook`, `answers` and `identity`, and
none is singled out. `rules` shows it: `homma rules about`, `homma rules render` and
`homma rules load` are the built-in's, and `homma rules show` is the tool's, so
the cards' pointer to the elaboration is a command that answers. `rules load`
prints for a session the cards it gets with their bytes, and with `--all` the
count and bytes for each role and place, so a check counts what each kind of
session loads rather than the whole corpus. A row counts the cards the session
loads at its start, those with no `paths:` scope, and reports the cards it
admits and loads only against matching files as their own count and bytes, so
the figure is comparable with the always-loaded set. `--all` and the options
that name one session exclude each other. A word that neither the built-in
group nor the tool knows is refused naming both sets of verbs. `tools` is not a
group in this sense, since `homma tools run <name>` is how any tool is named,
whatever a built-in holds.

A name that is no built-in and no promoted tool is refused saying so, and that
`homma tools` lists what there is, where clap would have said only that the
subcommand is unrecognised.

## Finding a tool

`homma what <words>` takes what an agent wants to do in its own words and answers
with the things that do it: the tool, the script, the homma verb, the mockspace
command, the skill or the rule, each with the exact command to run, a one line
summary, and the words of the query that made it match and where they matched. The
words are free text, quoted or not, and `homma what to use for landing a branch`
is the same query as `homma what landing a branch`, since the filler is dropped.
A query that matches nothing prints that and what could not be searched, and
exits non-zero, so a script can tell an answer from none. `--limit <n>` keeps the
first n answers, five unless given and never fewer than one, `--kind <kind>` keeps one kind and may be
repeated, and `--no-mock` leaves out the one source that starts a process.
`--output json` gives the same answer as data: the query, the terms it was
reduced to, the hits with their kind, name, command, summary, matched, score and
related names, and the gaps.

The sources are the places that already hold what a tool is for, read where they
sit, and nothing is copied into an index. A second list would go stale the way
every second list does, and the answer is then wrong with the authority of a
tool.

- A tool is a directory with a `tool.toml`, read the way `homma tools` reads it:
  the name, the summary, the tags, and each verb with its own summary. Its command
  is `homma <name>` where the tool is promoted and `homma tools run <name>` where
  it is not, followed by the verb that matched best, and where none matched and the tool has verbs but none named for itself, followed by its verbs in angle brackets (`homma disk-trim <trim|auto|report>`), since a tool with no default verb is refused without one. These exist on unix only,
  since renki builds there only, and off unix the footer says so.
  The tool's `README.md`, beside the descriptor, is read too, for a tool whose forms are written there
  and not declared one by one (`msg`, whose channel is an argument and whose verbs follow it). A line in a
  fenced block that begins with the tool's name or the name of one of its commands is a usage line, and a
  usage line is a verb: its name is the words after the command, its summary is the text set off by a
  column gap of two spaces or more, and its command is the line without that text, the tool's own name
  replaced by the command that dispatches it, so `msg kaski show 3f9a1c` is answered as `homma msg kaski show 3f9a1c`.
  The prose of the readme is the rest of the tool's text, weighed as the rest of a script's header is. A
  readme that is absent or unreadable is no gap, since a tool does not need one. At most 80 usage lines
  and 24000 characters of prose are kept from one readme, the prose stopping at the
  24000th character wherever that falls, inside a line included.
- A script is a file under `.shared/scripts`, at any depth, that can be run: it is
  executable, or it starts with a shebang line, in which case its command is the
  interpreter the line names followed by the path. A file with neither is a
  library and is not an answer, whatever its extension, and neither is one whose
  header has a line or a sentence beginning `Sourced`, which is how the hygiene directory's
  convention has a file say that another file sources it. So is a file that is not
  executable and that another script (one that is executable or opens with a shebang) sources, with `source`, `.` or a `shellcheck source=`
  line, or lists as a module in a `lib.nut`, since the workspace says it there. A test is not a script: a
  name with `_test`, `_fixture` or `_catalogue` in it or starting `test_`, a `test` runner beside
  its suite, and anything under a `tests` or `test` directory. A program's own
  source, under a `src` or `lib` directory, is a script only where it is
  executable. A symbolic link is not followed, whether to a file or to a
  directory, and the scripts directory itself being a link is a gap in the footer
  and not a way out of the workspace.
  Its descriptor is the comment block it opens with,
  read in place. The block is the run of comment lines after the shebang, in
  whichever spelling the language gives it: `#` lines, a `"""` docstring or a
  `/** */` block or `//` lines. Its first line of prose is the summary, with a leading
  `<name> - `, `<name>: ` or `<name>` and an em-dash taken off; a line that is only rules (`====`) or only the script's own name is
  skipped. A line after that one which begins with the script's own name is a usage line,
  and its second word, when it is a plain lowercase word set off by a single space
  and not by a column gap, names a verb the script takes. A `shellcheck` line is not part of the block. A line `tags: a, b` in the block adds tags. The directory the script
  sits in is its place, since `remote` and `hygiene` are how the workspace already
  groups them. The prose after the summary is kept as the rest of the header. A
  script with no such block has no descriptor and is counted, not guessed at. The
  directories `probes`, `.git`, `.venv`, `node_modules` and `__pycache__` are not read.
- A homma verb is a subcommand of homma, read from clap's own description of the
  command line, two levels deep, so `homma rules about` is found by its own name
  and its own help. These need no descriptor, since the command line is one.
- A mockspace command is a line of `cargo mock tools` in a repository of the
  workspace that has a `mockspace.toml`: the builtin subcommands and the project
  tools, with the kind the listing gives each. It is the one source that starts a
  process, one for each repository and side by side, with both its pipes drained as
  it runs and the whole process group ended when it overruns, the limit counting the time spent reading
  as well as waiting, so it is the only one `--no-mock` leaves out, it is run for no
  repository outside the instance's `repos` where that is given, and a repository
  where it fails is named in the footer. A listing is a run of `cargo mock`, which refreshes the
  hooks of its own repository as any run of it does, a side effect of this source and of no other. The builtins are the same in every repository and are
  listed once. A project tool belongs to one repository and runs nowhere else, so
  its place is that repository and its command is prefixed with `cd <repository> &&`.
- A skill is a directory under `.shared/skills`, with its description. Its command
  is the slash command that loads it, `/<name>`.
- A rule is a file under `.shared/rules`, with its `fires` line as the summary, its
  topics as tags and its card as the rest of its text. Its command is `homma rules show <name>`.

What a source cannot be read from is not an error. An absent directory is a source
with nothing in it, a local config that does not parse is read as naming no repository, so every repository is listed, and the footer says so,
a workspace with no mockspace repository has no such commands, and a source left
out by a flag is a source not read. The answer's footer says each, with the count of what it
could not describe: `12 scripts under .shared/scripts carry no header comment`,
`1 tool.toml cannot be read`, `mockspace commands not listed in muisti: cargo mock
tools failed`. The footer is the way a gap in the tooling becomes visible to the
agent that hit it, and it is printed whether or not anything matched.

How a query finds a thing. The text is lowercased, split into words at everything
that is not a letter or a digit, a single letter is dropped, and so is the filler, as it is or with its plural taken off (so `note`, `noted` and `notes` keep their subject, which `not` would not), a short fixed list of
the words that carry no subject (`a`, `the`, `to`, `for`, `use`, `what`, `which`,
`how`, `do`, `i`, `my`, `this`, and the like). What remains is reduced to stems
the same way the sources' text is, by taking off a plural, then `ing` or `ed`, so a root such as `speed` is cut the same bare or plural, and the
like, so `landed`, `lands` and `landing` are one stem. A word is looked for in
seven places, each with its own weight, from the most to the least: the name, the
tags, the verb names, the summary, the verb summaries, the place (the
directories a script sits in, the repository a mockspace command comes from) and
the rest of a script's header. A rule is named by a sentence, so the words of a
rule's name count as its summary and not as a name. For each word the best way it
matches in the best place wins, and a match is one of these, in order of what it
is worth: the same stem; a word of the same group in the small table of synonyms
(`free`, `trim`, `reclaim`, `clean` are one group, `done`, `finished`, `complete`
another, `end`, `ended`, `stop` a third, and no word is in two groups once stemmed); a prefix of at least four letters; a misspelling, which is one edit for
a word of five to seven letters and two for eight or more, and for a shorter word
only two letters swapped (`dsik` for `disk`), since any other edit in `bake`
makes `make`. A word is worth more the fewer things it matches, by the usual
inverse document weighting over the corpus read this time, so `branch`, which
names half the tooling, does not decide an answer that `landed` or `lost` could.

A thing scores the sum over the query's words of what each is worth in it, scaled
by a factor between a half and one that grows with the share of the query's
weight it matched, so a thing that matches the rare words of a query beats one
that matches the common ones, and then by what its kind
is worth against the others: a tool, a script, a homma verb and a mockspace
command count in full, a skill four fifths and a rule three fifths, since a rule
is advice beside the tools and shares words with them by chance. A thing that
matched fewer of the words than half of them (both, from a query of two, and a third of them, from a query of
five or more) is not an answer, and neither is one that scores under a fifth of
the best, so a query about nothing the tooling does returns nothing, and
returns it with a non-zero exit. Ties break by kind (tool, script, homma verb,
mockspace command, skill, rule), then by name, so the order is the same on every
run.

Related things are the other tools, scripts, verbs and mockspace commands that
sit with a match, or the other skills and rules where the match is advice: those
whose words are closest to its own, the cosine of the two bags of words (name,
tags, place, summary and verb names, weighted the same way) being at least
0.225, so a tool is named beside `disk-trim` for being about the same thing and
not for sharing a tag every tool carries. The figure is set on a catalogue of pairs of the
workspace that must be named together and pairs that must not, kept in the `calibration` test
of the acceptance set: it lies above the nearest pair that must not be named together (0.216)
and at or below the nearest that must (0.233), and the test fails when a pair crosses it. Up to three are named under each of the
first three answers, never one that is already an answer.

The synonym table is a vocabulary of the tooling, a list of groups of words, and
adding a group is a data change with a test beside it. It is not a list of
tools, and no tool is named in it.

The acceptance of the command is a set of queries run against the workspace's
real tools and scripts, never against a fixture made to pass: each query is what
a helper or the coordinator would type and names the thing that must come first,
and one query must return nothing. The set lives with the command's tests and
reads the workspace the repository sits in; where there is no workspace around
the repository the set is refused, not skipped. It is run with every source, the mockspace listing among them, since
that source can push a tool from first place. A query the matcher does not yet
answer is kept as an ignored case that says what should come first, and the
work's report says the requirement behind it is unmet.

The instance's scope (`repos` under `[instance]` in `homma.local.toml`) is kept
where it applies: the tools, scripts, skills and rules belong to the workspace
and are read whole, and the mockspace commands are read only for the repositories
the scope names. A listing that takes longer than a minute is given up on and
named in the footer.

## The agent hooks

An agent session opened at the workspace root loads the workspace's own
`.claude/settings.json` and `.claude/settings.local.json` and nothing from the
repositories under it, so every
hook a repository's mockspace generates reaches such a session only if homma
carries it up. `homma agent regen` does that, and it owes the same thing to
those hooks that git-shook owes to git's: every one of them keeps running, at
the event and for the tools it was written for, and nothing homma did not write
is replaced or removed.

What the regen writes belongs to the clone it ran in, since a clone holds
whichever members it holds and the hooks follow them. So its registrations go
to `.claude/settings.local.json`, which the host reads per checkout and merges
over `settings.json`, and its wrappers to `.claude/hooks/`, and a workspace
tracks neither. Two clones with different members then write two different
sets and neither can take the other's away. `settings.json` stays what every
clone shares, written by hand, and the regen writes nothing into it: a
registration of its own that it finds there is one an earlier regen put in the
shared file, and it is swept, whichever member it names and whether or not
this clone holds it, while every other one is kept as it is. A registration
is homma's by its name: a hook under `.claude/hooks/` named `<repo>--<rest>`,
with something on both sides, is in homma's namespace whether or not its file
is present, and one somebody wrote by hand takes another name or another
directory, since a file homma did not write under such a name is the only
thing that keeps it. Nothing in
`settings.local.json` that is not homma's is touched, since that file also
holds what the person at that clone allowed and prefers. Both files are read
and swept before either is written, so one that does not parse leaves both as
they were, and the local file is written first, so a failure between the two
writes leaves a registration twice rather than nowhere.

The host runs every hook registered for an event, in parallel, and runs a
handler named twice only once. So the dispatcher git needs, one program at an
event fanning out to many, is not needed here, and one registration per hook is
what homma writes. A dispatcher would put a homma process in front of every
tool call and serialise hooks the host already runs side by side, to buy an
order nothing here depends on.

What is homma's is decided by what homma wrote. Every file it writes under
`.claude/hooks/` carries a line saying it was written by `homma agent regen`,
and a registration is homma's only when the file it names carries that line.
Anything else is kept exactly as it is, and a file homma would have to overwrite
to write its own is refused by path, with the regen reporting it and exiting
`1`. A name alone decides nothing, since a hand-written hook can carry any name.

Each repository hook is registered under every event and matcher its
repository's `settings.json` gives it, and under the matchers its own
`@matchers:` line names where the repository gives none. A hook that has
neither is not written as a wrapper nobody calls: it is reported by path, and
the regen exits `1`. Any executable file under the repository's `.claude/hooks/`
is a hook, whatever language it is in, and a file there that is not executable
and that no registration runs through a program is reported the same way, since
the host could not run it either.

A workspace declares hooks of its own in the manifest, under `[agent.hooks]`,
one array per event named as the host names it:

```toml
[[agent.hooks.PreToolUse]]
matcher = "Bash"
run     = "scripts/keep-the-build-off-this-machine"
repos   = ["engine"]
```

`run` is a path under the workspace, and `repos` narrows the hook to calls
landing in those repositories, the way every aggregated hook is narrowed to its
own. Without it the hook sees every call. homma writes a wrapper for each row
beside the aggregated ones, carrying the same line, so the rows are kept and
removed by the same rule, and a guard a workspace wants in every session is a
line in the manifest rather than a hand edit to a generated file.

The table is read from the same layers as the git hooks: the manifest, then
each overlay file its `layers` key names, `homma.local.toml` where the key is
absent. A row in an overlay is appended after the manifest's for its event and
replaces none of them, so a clone adds a guard of its own and cannot remove a
shared one. A registration written from an overlay row is the clone's, which is
where the regen already writes, so it lands in `settings.local.json` like every
other.

A call is narrowed by where it lands, and a hook sees it when any place it
lands is under the hook's repository. For a tool writing a file that is the
file's path, and a path that climbs with `..` lands everywhere. For a shell call it is the directory the host says the session is
in, and every path the command names as well, read relative to that directory,
since `cargo test --manifest-path engine/Cargo.toml` typed at the workspace
root lands in `engine` whatever directory it was typed in. Where the host says
nothing about a directory, the hook process's own working directory stands in,
and that is the workspace root whatever the session did.

A wrapper is not a shell, so it reads a command by splitting it on everything
that cannot be part of a path, which takes quotes, separators, redirections and
the `=` in `--flag=path` apart, and reading each piece as a path. It also looks
for a repository's absolute root anywhere in the command, and a piece that
climbs with `..` counts as landing everywhere, since a guard skipped costs what
the guard is for. A guard run once too often costs a process where the guard
only observes. Where it denies, it costs a false refusal: a bare word equal to a
repository's path, as in `echo engine`, lands there, and a guard that denies on
anything it is handed denies a call that touched nothing of its repository.
What the wrapper cannot see is a relative path with a space in it, a path
spelled through a variable, and whatever a script the command runs goes on to
do. A guard that needs those reads the command itself.

A hook the wrapper runs is run in the directory the session is in, and handed
the payload exactly as the host sent it. Running it from its repository's root
instead would satisfy a hook that asks its own working directory where it is,
and would break every hook that reads a relative path out of the payload, since
that path is relative to the session's directory. So the wrapper reaching a hook
decides only that the hook runs, and what it then allows is the hook's own
reading of the payload. A hook that scopes itself by its working directory
declines a call typed elsewhere that names its repository by path, however the
wrapper routed it, and scoping by the payload's directory and the command's
paths is what makes it see one.

A registration names its hook by the first word of its command that reaches
into `.claude/hooks/`. Whatever precedes that word is the program the host runs
it with, as in `bash .claude/hooks/check.sh`, and whatever follows is its
arguments, both of which the wrapper carries. A hook run through a program does
not have to be executable. A hook that two registrations run through two
different programs is reported by path and not carried, since one wrapper can
only run it one way. So is a hook whose program is a line of shell rather than a
program, an assignment in front of it or a separator before it, since the
wrapper runs a program with arguments and a shell line would run as something
else: `cd dir && .claude/hooks/x.sh` would run a `cd` binary and report its
success as the hook's.

A file under a repository's `.claude/hooks/` whose name starts with a dot is an
editor's or a filesystem's, and is neither carried nor reported. A registration
in the workspace naming a file homma writes, where that file is no longer
there, is removed on the next regen, since nothing it could run exists.

## What a status answers

`homma status` answers what state the workspace is in. That question is best
served by what is wrong rather than by an inventory of what is fine, so it covers
every population homma can report and shows, by default, only what is not in the
state it should be in. A repository with nothing to say about it takes one line.
`--full` prints every population whole, for when that is what somebody wants.

The narrower verbs are unchanged and stay the place to see one population
entirely, healthy entries included. With the plain status there is no need to
know which of them to reach for.

A workspace builds into one `target/` at its root. A second build directory, a
worktree's own or one a test wrote under a crate, costs another copy of every
dependency and is a state worth reporting, so the status walks the workspace for
them and lists each with its size. A directory counts when it is called `target`
and holds a `CACHEDIR.TAG`, a `.rustc_info.json`, or a `debug` or `release`
directory, so a source directory with that name is left out. The walk goes six
levels down and does not enter a build directory it found, `.git` or
`node_modules`, and follows no link. The human output prints the section only
where there is something in it, and the document carries the list always, empty
where there is nothing. Nothing is deleted.

Covered: the workspace and its forges, what this clone of it is for, the agent
surfaces including whether the repository's git hooks are wired at all, the
shared tool configs, the working tree, and the build directories under the
workspace other than its one `target/`. The doc surfaces are deliberately not, being a survey of what a repository
has rather than a report of anything being wrong, and nothing offline is left
out for cost. Nothing here reaches the network, which is what makes it the
command to run first.

What the agent surfaces ask of a repository depends on whether it has adopted
mockspace, which is whether it carries a `mockspace.toml`, at its root or under
`mock/`, and by that alone. A repository with a mock directory and no
`mockspace.toml`, mockspace's own tree among them, has not adopted it, any more
than one with neither has. Neither is short of anything: each is named as having
no mockspace under `--full` and nowhere else, and `homma agent regen` skips the
`cargo mock` stage for each, since `cargo mock` has nothing to run there. The
shared configs are still compared and placed for every repository, adopted or
not. One that has adopted is asked for its mock directory, as its
`mockspace.toml` declares it with `mock` as the default, for the rendered
`.claude/` and `.github/instructions/`, and for the wiring that makes its git
hooks fire. It is not asked for an `agent/` directory under its mock directory,
because the builtin rules ship inside mockspace and that directory holds only
what the repository adds, and it is not asked for a `cargo mock` alias, because
the launcher answers that command from where it is installed. A line about a
missing surface names `homma agent regen`, which renders them. A repository
that is not on disk is named as not being there, with no surfaces listed and no
command to run.

The machine-readable document carries one probe per surface that is asked for,
among them whether the toml carries the launcher pin, which the commit gate
asks and the summary does not, and none for a surface that is not, so it has no field for the agent directory
under the mock directory or for the `cargo mock` alias. The document is never
summarised, whatever the human output is doing: a document that quietly omitted the healthy members would be
misdescribing the population to the one reader with no way to notice.

## What a clone is for

The manifest is committed and every clone of the content repository shares it,
so it says only what is true of the workspace everywhere. The same content
repository is cloned once per body of work, and what one clone is for is a fact
about that clone alone. It lives in `homma.local.toml`, beside the manifest and
never committed.

Two tables. `[instance]` is homma's and typed: `work` names the body of work and
is the one key required, `title` is what to call it, `repos` narrows which
members the work spans and means all of them when empty, and `state` and `goal`
are paths from the workspace root to the document carrying the work's current
state and the one carrying what it is trying to reach. The file points at those
documents and decides nothing about them; where a workspace keeps its state and
which goal is current are its own conventions. `[tools.<name>]` belongs to the
workspace's tools and is carried untyped, for the reason `[[status.inject]]`
runs a tool without knowing it: homma holds a tool's settings without growing
an opinion about them.

The manifest's loader does not read it. Only `status` and `local` use the file,
and they read it themselves, anchored beside the manifest, because every other
command, the commit and push gates among them, has nothing to learn from it and
no reason to refuse over it. Absent is the ordinary case and nothing notices.
Present and not parsing is never silently ignored either: `status` gives it a
line of its own naming the file and the error, and `local show` fails on it.

`homma local init --work <name>` writes the file with every key in it, refuses
to overwrite one that exists, and adds `/homma.local.toml` and
`/homma.local.toml.*` to the workspace's `.gitignore`, each where no line names
it yet: a file described as never committed that is one `git add -A` from being
committed is a file that will be, and the second line covers the lock and the
temporary file `set` leaves behind when it dies halfway.
`homma local show` prints it, the document whole with `--output json`, so a
script reads it through homma's parser rather than a second one; no file is
exit `1`, a command that ran and reported. `homma local set <table.key> <value>`
writes one string under `instance` or under `tools.<name>` and nowhere else,
keeps every comment around it, and refuses a result that would not parse. It
exists so a hook can record what it learned without anybody editing the file,
and a hook runs beside whatever else reads the file, so the read, edit and write
happen under a lock file beside it, created exclusively and retried for a
bounded time before `set` refuses, and the result goes to a temporary file in
the same directory renamed over the original. A reader sees the old file or the
new one and never a half, and two writers at once both land.

A workspace keeps one config per body of work, so a fresh clone does not start
from `init` and a hand-filled skeleton. They are stored in the content
repository as `.shared/local/<work>.toml`, each a whole `homma.local.toml` named
for the work it is for, and `homma local install <name>` puts the named one in
place, as a copy, because a link would make every write a tool records, a publish
digest or a deployed commit, an edit of a tracked file, and `set` renames a
temporary file over the path, which replaces a link with a plain file at the
first write without a word. So the split between what is declared and what is
recorded is by where each lives: the stored file holds what is declared, the
installed copy is the clone's own and takes what tools record, and nothing
flows back. A value that proves durable is moved into the store by editing the
stored file. Homma holds no list of which keys are which. A stored file's name
is its `instance.work`, which is also how the installed file says which config
it came from, and one whose two differ is refused. Where there is no file
`install` writes the stored one byte for byte and ignores it as `init` does.
Where one exists for the same work it adds the keys it lacks and changes none it
has, so running it again is safe and a goal switched on locally stays on; lists
are values and are not merged into, and a table is entered whether the file
writes it as a `[table]` or inline, so a key inside `goal = { active = "true" }`
is added when it is missing and reported like any other. Where the file is for another work, does not
parse, or is a link, it refuses and says why, and `--replace` writes the stored one beside it and then moves the old one aside
to `homma.local.toml.replaced` instead of deleting it, refusing if that name is
taken, so a write that fails leaves the old file where it was. `homma local list` names the stored configs sorted, marks the one whose
work the installed file has, shows a stored file that cannot be installed with
the reason instead of leaving it out, whether it is the name that `install` would
refuse, a name that is not UTF-8, or the content, and exits `1` where there is no
store; the document carries the same as a list of rows, empty where there is no
store.

`homma status` carries the instance, one line in the human output and the
table whole in the document, `null` where there is no file. Where the file does
not parse the instance is `null` and `instance_error` carries the message.

A file names a console when `[tools.console]` carries a non-empty `channel`, and a
clone that does keeps no dashboard, the console being its replacement. Homma
holds no opinion on what a dashboard is, so what it does about that is limited
to the file, and it is one check, run over the text that every write produces: a
result that names a console and also carries a `tools.dashboard` table is
refused, whichever key made it so. `local set` refuses a key under
`tools.dashboard` in such a file, which would otherwise put a recorded page back
into a clone that has none, and the `channel` of a console in a file that
still holds a dashboard. `local install` into an existing clone fills the
stored config's missing keys through the same check, so a clone keeping its old
`[tools.dashboard]` is not given a console beside it, and a stored config that
holds both is refused, so a fresh clone is never started from the contradiction.
The tool that builds the page reads the same fact from the parsed file and
refuses its own verbs.

An injected tool that prints nothing and does not fail gets no heading in the
human output of `homma status`, so a tool that has switched itself off leaves no
empty line behind. A tool that failed keeps its heading, and the document
carries every block either way.
